Settings.General.UseMetadata.Hint=Some file formats have metadata with additional information about the author, title, publisher, producer and so on. NBR can read and display that information where it exists. However, if that information is wrong or missing, NBR can take the basic details from folder and file names instead. As a last resort, you can always rename books in the Library.
Settings.General.UseMultimediaKeys.Hint=You can use your keyboard multimedia keys or headset keys to play, pause and navigate the book using the navigation step you have chosen.\n\nThe second switch takes them system-wide, so they reach NBR from anywhere — at the price of taking them from every other player while NBR is running.
Settings.General.LibraryLocation.Hint=This is the place where NBR copies imported books. You can leave it as default on your system partition, but if you plan to have a large library, another partition or disk would be a much better solution. NBR also keeps the information for each book in there, so if you want to save your reading positions, bookmarks, speed and other reading settings, note that you need to copy the content from the old library folder manually to the new location. And within the same price range: by changing the library location to somewhere other than the system partition, you are preserving your books from accidental system crashes and the usual data loss accompanying them.
Settings.General.Language.Restart=Changing the language needs NBR to start again. Restart now?
Settings.General.Language.RestartTitle=Language
Settings.General.UpdateGroup=Updates
Settings.General.AutoCheckUpdates=Check for updates automatically
Settings.General.AutoCheckUpdates.Hint=With this on, NBR asks once a day whether a newer version has been published, and speaks up only if there is one. Nothing about you or your books is sent: the request carries no identifier, no library and no list of what you read. Turn it off if you would rather NBR never reached the network on its own — you can still ask whenever you like, with Help, Check for update, in the library window.
Settings.Misc.Look=Look:
Settings.Misc.Look.Classic=System colors
Settings.Misc.Look.New=NBR design
Settings.Misc.Look.Hint=How NBR draws itself, and there are two answers.\n\nNBR design is its own look, and the one it starts with.\n\nSystem colors makes it a plain window in whatever colors and fonts Windows is set to. It paints nothing of its own, so your own scheme comes through whole — which is what you want with a high contrast theme, and what works best with a magnifier. If you already have high contrast switched on, this is the one NBR starts with.\n\nEither way the choice is yours to make and yours to undo: the keyboard and your screen reader are unaffected by it, and F2 brings you back here.
