Services.Disclaimer=THE STEPS BELOW MAY NOT MATCH WHAT YOU SEE. These services redesign their web sites without warning, so a button may have moved or been renamed since this was written. The ORDER of what has to be done rarely changes; the names of things do. If you get lost, the part above tells you what you are trying to achieve.
Services.Item.GoogleVoices=Google Cloud voices
Services.Item.AzureVoices=Azure Speech voices
Services.Item.DeepSeek=DeepSeek translation
Services.Item.Gemini=Gemini translation
Services.Item.OpenAi=GPT translation (OpenAI)
Services.About.Gemini=Gemini translates a whole book into another language, using Google's own model. Judged the best of them by ear — and the one with a catch.\n\nIt refuses roughly every sixth passage of a published novel. Not for the content: the pattern looks like the model recognising a book it knows, and no setting changes it. That is why NBR asks a second service for whatever the first will not take, and why the chain is worth setting up rather than leaving to one engine.\n\nThere is a free tier and it is generous enough to be the normal way to use this: measured on a real project, Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite allows 500 requests a day, which is about six books. Paid use costs roughly twenty-five cents for a 450,000-character novel.\n\nONE THING TO WEIGH BEFORE CHOOSING FREE. Google's own pricing page says that on the free tier your content is used to improve their products, and that on the paid tier it is not. A translation sends the whole book, so that is a real difference. It is your choice either way — but it should be a choice and not a surprise.\n\nGetting the key is the longest of the four, and the steps below are written from a real run because the obvious route does not work.
Services.About.GoogleVoices=Google Cloud voices read books aloud over the internet, in Google's own voices. They are the best of the cloud voices for most languages, and the free allowance is the largest — around a million characters a month, which is about two books.\n\nThree things have to happen, in this order. You need a GOOGLE ACCOUNT, which most people already have. Inside it you make a PROJECT and switch the Text-to-Speech service on for that project — and Google will not switch it on until billing is set up, so a card is needed even though the free allowance costs nothing. Then you make a SERVICE ACCOUNT and download its key.\n\nThat last part is what makes this one unusual. Google does not hand out a key you can paste: it gives a small file ending in .json, downloaded once. NBR keeps what is inside that file rather than the file itself, so you can move or delete the download afterwards.
Services.About.AzureVoices=Azure Speech voices read books aloud over the internet, in Microsoft's voices. They are very good in English and many other languages. For Croatian they are ordinary, and Azure does not support the pronunciation dictionary there, so a rule you write for a name will not be obeyed. The free allowance is about half a million characters a month, roughly one book.\n\nTwo things have to happen, and only the first is yours. You need a MICROSOFT ACCOUNT before you can have an Azure one, and then an AZURE SUBSCRIPTION — which needs identity checks, a card and an agreement. A person has to do that deliberately, so it cannot be automated and NBR does not try.\n\nEverything after that is NBR's job. It signs in with a short code, creates the resource on the free tier, and keeps the key. You never open the Azure portal.\n\nOne thing catches people out. When your subscription is made, Azure quietly creates a DIRECTORY for it. If your Microsoft account is a personal one — Gmail, Outlook, Hotmail — Azure refuses the sign-in unless you name that directory, because it does not recognise personal accounts for this. Its name ends in onmicrosoft.com.
Services.About.DeepSeek=DeepSeek translates a whole book into another language. Of the services NBR can use it is the steadiest — it refuses least often — and it is far the cheapest.\n\nOne thing has to happen: an account with DeepSeek, and some credit on it. There is no free allowance, but translation is charged by the amount of text and a book costs very little.\n\nThis one gives a key you paste, and it is the simplest of the four. The one thing to watch is that the key is shown ONCE, when you make it. If you close that window without copying it, it cannot be shown again and you simply make another.
Services.About.OpenAi=OpenAI translates a whole book into another language, using the GPT models. It is offered in three tiers on one account and one key: Terra, which is the one to start with, Luna, which is cheaper, and Sol, which is the dearest.\n\nThere is no free allowance of any kind, and this is the part people are caught by: a ChatGPT subscription does NOT pay for this. The API is billed separately, and a new account is PREPAID — you put a small amount of credit on it, and calls spend from that balance. Five or ten dollars is plenty to see what a book really costs before deciding anything.\n\nWhat a book costs, worked out for a 450 000-character novel and using the way Croatian actually breaks into tokens rather than a rule of thumb: roughly two and a half dollars on Terra, one dollar on Luna, five on Sol. For comparison, the same book measured at about twenty-three cents on Gemini. Any of them is small for a book you care about; Terra is around ten times Gemini for a habit.\n\nThe key is a plain one you paste in here, which makes this the simplest of the services to set up.
Services.Steps.Gemini=DO NOT USE AI STUDIO FOR THIS. Its "Create API key" button fails with "The request is suspicious. Please try again." — measured on a fresh account that had never had a payment method, with the project already created and imported. The real error underneath is a 403 from Google's own service. Everything below is in the CLOUD CONSOLE instead, and that route works.\n\nThe order matters: the project, then the API, then a service account, and only then the key. Done in that order the key is made in a single pass.\n\n1. Open console.cloud.google.com/projectcreate in your browser and sign in.\n\n2. Give the project a name you will recognise, such as NBR. Leave Organization set to "No organization" — that is the normal answer for a personal account, and an organization brings policies you do not control. Select CREATE and wait for the project to appear.\n\n3. IF GOOGLE OFFERS A FREE TRIAL WITH 300 DOLLARS OF CREDIT, DECLINE IT. That credit does not pay for the Gemini API at all — it is a different wallet for a different product. Worse, accepting it creates a billing account, and Google then links billing to every new project automatically, which takes the project OFF the free tier. It also asks for a payment card. Say no.\n\n4. Open console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/generativelanguage.googleapis.com. Check that the project name shown in the bar across the top is the one you just made. The page is headed "Gemini API". Select ENABLE and wait until the button disappears.\n\n5. Open console.cloud.google.com/iam-admin/serviceaccounts/create. This is the step that saves you doing the next one twice: the key cannot reach Gemini unless it is bound to a service account, and making it now means it is waiting when you need it.\n\n6. On the page headed "Create service account", fill only the first field, "Service account name" — nbr-translate is a good name, and the ID underneath fills itself. Select "CREATE AND CONTINUE". Step 2, "Permissions", is optional: leave it empty, no role is needed. Select DONE at the bottom.\n\n7. Open console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials. Check the project name in the top bar again. In the row of actions beside the heading "Credentials", select "+ CREATE CREDENTIALS", and from the menu that drops down choose the FIRST item, "API key" — not "OAuth client ID" and not "Service account".\n\n8. A panel opens on the right, headed "Create API key". In the "Name" field at the very top, type a name such as NBR.\n\n9. Scroll DOWN inside that panel, past the section "APIs that can be accessed using this key", to a check box labelled "Authenticate API calls through a service account". TICK IT. This is the step nobody guesses: until it is ticked, Gemini cannot be chosen at all — its entry stays greyed out and only a tooltip says why.\n\n10. A section headed "Service account" appears just below. Select "+ Select a service account", choose the account you made in step 6 by selecting the circle at the left of its row, and select SELECT at the bottom of that window.\n\n11. Now scroll UP in the panel to "APIs that can be accessed using this key" and open the list under "Select API restrictions". "Gemini API" can be ticked now. Tick it and select OK.\n\n12. Scroll DOWN to the bottom of the panel and select CREATE. Leave "Application restrictions" set to "None".\n\n13. A window headed "API key created" appears with the key in it. It begins with AQ. rather than AIza, which is correct for a key bound to a service account. Copy it with the copy icon at the right of the field.\n\n14. Come back here, select Set up this service, and paste the key. NBR checks it with Google before keeping it.\n\nIF THE CONSOLE SAYS YOU NEED ADDITIONAL ACCESS and names a missing permission, you are almost certainly signed in to the wrong Google account rather than short of rights. Add authuser=1 to the end of the address — or authuser=2, and so on — until the project appears.
Services.Steps.GoogleVoices=1. Open console.cloud.google.com in your browser and sign in with a Google account.\n\n2. Accept the terms if you are asked, and create a project when it offers to. Any name will do.\n\n3. Find the search box at the top, type "Cloud Text-to-Speech API", choose it from the results and press Enable.\n\n4. Google will ask you to set up billing before it enables the API. Add a card.\n\n5. Search again for "Service accounts" and open it. Press Create service account, give it any name, and press Done. No role or permission needs choosing.\n\n6. Open the account you just made, go to the Keys tab, press Add key, then Create new key, and choose JSON. The file downloads.\n\n7. Come back here, press "Set up this service", and choose that downloaded file.
Services.Steps.AzureVoices=1. Open azure.microsoft.com/free in your browser and press Start free.\n\n2. Sign in with a Microsoft account, or make one.\n\n3. Fill in your details. Azure asks for a card to prove you are a person; the free tier is not charged.\n\n4. If your account is a personal one, find your directory name: sign in to the portal and it is shown in the address bar after the @ sign, ending in onmicrosoft.com.\n\n5. Come back here and press "Set up this service".\n\n6. Fill in that directory name if your account is a personal one, and leave it empty if it is a work or school account. Press Start.\n\n7. Open the sign-in page with the button, type the short code shown, and sign in.\n\n8. Choose your subscription and press Create. NBR makes the resource and keeps its key. You type nothing else.
Services.Steps.DeepSeek=1. Open platform.deepseek.com in your browser.\n\n2. Press Sign up. You can fill in an e-mail and password, or choose to sign up with Google, which is quicker.\n\n3. Confirm your e-mail if you are asked to.\n\n4. Once signed in, find Top up or Billing and add a small amount of credit.\n\n5. Find API keys in the menu on the left.\n\n6. Press Create new API key, give it any name, and press Create.\n\n7. Copy the key BEFORE you close that window.\n\n8. Come back here, press "Set up this service", and paste it in.
Services.Steps.OpenAi=1. Open platform.openai.com in your browser. This is the developer platform, not the ChatGPT site, and it is a separate thing even if you already pay for ChatGPT.\n\n2. Sign in with your account, or make one.\n\n3. FIRST check which organization you are in. The name sits at the top left of the page and it is a menu: open it and choose your own, which is usually called Personal. If you were ever invited into somebody else's organization, or you have a ChatGPT Team or Business seat through work, the platform can put you in THAT one — where you are only a member, billing refuses you with "required permission: api.billing.read", and any key you made would belong to the wrong account. Everything below has to happen in your own organization.\n\n4. Go to platform.openai.com/settings/organization/billing/overview. Billing sits under ORGANIZATION, not under the plain settings page, which is where it is easy to look for it and not find it. Select Add to balance, add a payment card, and buy some credit. Ten dollars is the minimum and more than enough to begin. Nothing works until there is credit on the account.\n\n5. While you are there, set a monthly spending limit. It costs nothing and it means a mistake cannot run up a bill.\n\n6. Open API keys in the same settings, and select Create new secret key. Give it a name you will recognise, such as NBR. Make sure you are still in the same organization as the credit.\n\n7. Copy the key the moment it appears. It is shown ONCE. If you close that window without copying it, it cannot be shown again — you simply delete it and make another.\n\n8. Come back here, select Set up this service, and paste the key.\n\n9. NBR checks the key with OpenAI before keeping it, so you find out straight away whether it works.

